"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][40]={"university_physics_en:M40-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M40-L1 – CT Projection Data, the Radon Transform and Reconstruction Limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M40 – Master: Advanced Medical Physics and Quality Assurance<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M24: imaging methods; M31: inverse\nproblems; M39 · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret logarithmic CT\nprojections as line integrals and explain principal limits of\nbackprojection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Computed tomography does not directly measure finished slices. The\ndetector acquires projections at many angles; an inverse algorithm\nconstructs a spatial map of attenuation coefficients. A sinogram\norganizes detector position and view angle; it is not itself an\nanatomical image.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal monoenergetic, scatter-free ray,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=\\exp(-\\int_\\ell\\mu(\\mathbf r)\\,d\\ell)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking the negative logarithm linearizes the path integral and provides\na sample of the Radon transform. Filtered backprojection boosts high\nspatial frequencies before backprojection; noise, finite detector\nresolution, beam hardening and missing views limit the result. The\nsimple logarithmic model is only approximate for polychromatic x-ray\nspectra.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(s)=-\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{I_\\theta(s)}{I_0(s)}\\right)=\\int_{\\ell(\\theta,s)}\\mu(\\mathbf r)\\,d\\ell.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>An illustrative ray crosses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof material with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_1=0.10\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand another\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_2=0.20\\,\\mathrm{cm^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe projection is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.6703<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=e^{-0.4}\\approx0.6703<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows a ray crossing two regions and a schematic angular\nacquisition.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_296_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic CT ray traversing two attenuation regions with angular projections.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic CT ray traversing two\nattenuation regions with angular projections.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ideal monochromatic projection model.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>No patient data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif the first path length doubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can missing projection angles create direction-dependent\nartifacts?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.7<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.4966<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-0.7}\\approx0.4966<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Some spatial-frequency information is unmeasured, making the\ninversion underdetermined.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation produces one projection measurement, not a full CT\nimage or dose estimate. Conceptual filtering does not replace spectral\ndetector, scatter or calibration corrections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The next lesson examines frequency and spatial encoding in MRI, where\nthe data-acquisition mechanism is fundamentally different. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M40&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L1 – CT Projection Data, the Radon Transform and Reconstruction Limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/w7qp0-nph35\">IAEA INIS – Digital Imaging, Chapter 16<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L1 – CT Projection Data, the Radon Transform and Reconstruction Limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jmri.24687\">Hansen &amp; Kellman – Image Reconstruction: An Overview for Clinicians<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L1 – CT Projection Data, the Radon Transform and Reconstruction Limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M40-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M40-L2 – MRI Gradient Encoding, Larmor Frequency and k-Space<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M40 – Master: Advanced Medical Physics and Quality Assurance<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M24: MRI foundations; M33: magnetic\nresonance and spectroscopy; M40-L1.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish angular frequency,\nordinary frequency and spatial k-space coordinates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In MRI, magnetic moments precess in a static field. An added linear\ngradient links position to resonance frequency or accumulated phase. A\nspatial image is reconstructed from time-sampled complex signals. Larmor\nfrequency alone does not determine spatial resolution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(x)=B_0+G_xx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the gyromagnetic ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpressed in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{rad\\,s^{-1}\\,T^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega(x)=\\gamma B(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOrdinary frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=\\omega/(2\\pi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a constant gradient direction and Fourier convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-i2\\pi k_xx}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe spatial-frequency coordinate can be defined as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_x(t)=\\gamma(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int_0^tG_x(t\\prime)dt\\prime<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin cycles per metre. Signal-acquisition signs and offsets depend on\nsequence conventions. The published correction to a nuclide table in an\nearlier overview is not used here as a numerical source.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>γ<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>γ<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f=\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\pi}G_xx,\\qquad k_x(t)=\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\pi}\\int_0^tG_x(t\\prime)\\,dt\\prime.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Use the standard approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>42.58<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma/(2\\pi)\\approx42.58\\,\\mathrm{MHz\\,T^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor hydrogen nuclei as a physical model parameter. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_x=10\\,\\mathrm{mT\\,m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8516<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f\\approx8516\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter a constant gradient duration of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{ms}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe magnitude of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>426<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_x\\approx426\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese numbers represent distinct quantities.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_184_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Linear spatial gradient producing position-dependent frequency with schematic k-space sampling.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Linear spatial gradient producing\nposition-dependent frequency with schematic k-space\nsampling.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Temporal and spatial frequency scales shown separately; not an\nacquired MR scan.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the frequency shift at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does one k-space sample not determine a complete image?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4258<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4258\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Cycles per metre\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">One sample carries only one component of the spatial-frequency\ninformation; imaging needs sufficient sampling and reconstruction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The model ignores relaxation, field inhomogeneity, receive coils and\ngradient ramps. Illustrative gradients are not clinical sequence or\nsafety recommendations; resolution additionally requires FOV, bandwidth\nand sampling assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>MRI primarily uses phase encoding, while the counting statistics of\ndigital x-ray detectors rests on discrete quantum events. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M40&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jmri.23642\">Plewes &amp; Kucharczyk – Physics of MRI: A Primer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L2 – MRI Gradient Encoding, Larmor Frequency and k-Space · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jmri.24687\">Hansen &amp; Kellman – Image Reconstruction: An Overview for Clinicians<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L2 – MRI Gradient Encoding, Larmor Frequency and k-Space · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M40-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M40-L3 – Digital Detectors, Poisson Noise and Image Quality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M40 – Master: Advanced Medical Physics and Quality Assurance<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: probability distributions; M32:\nmeasurement noise; M40-L2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish idealized counting\nstatistics of quantitative detector signals from resolution and\nartifacts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Digital images emerge from a measurement chain of interaction,\ndetection, electronics, sampling and processing. More registered quanta\ncan reduce relative counting noise, but cannot fix a broad point-spread\nfunction or systematic calibration errors. Pixels are not necessarily\nindependently measured spatial samples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal Poisson counter with expected event count\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Var}(n)=N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_n=\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen background and electronic noise are negligible and signal is\nproportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SNR}\\approx N/\\sqrt N=\\sqrt N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSpatial resolution is constrained separately by detector geometry,\npoint-spread function and system motion. Real detectors add noise\nsources and correlations.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_n=\\sqrt{N},\\qquad \\mathrm{SNR}_{\\mathrm{Poisson}}\\approx\\sqrt{N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>An illustrative measurement window registers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevents on average. Its counting standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>20<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand ideal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SNR}=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1600<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1600<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nSNR doubles to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>40<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neven though mean event count quadruples. The figure plots this\nsquare-root law. A change in registered events cannot be directly\nidentified with patient dose without acquisition geometry and\nconditions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_070_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Signal-to-noise ratio plotted as a square-root function of registered quanta with two marked examples.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Signal-to-noise ratio plotted as a\nsquare-root function of registered quanta with two marked\nexamples.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Independent ideal Poisson events.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not the response curve of a clinical detector.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is ideal SNR at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>900<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=900<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By what factor must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincrease for a threefold SNR increase?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do extra quanta not correct an unsuitable detector point-spread\nfunction?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>30<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By a factor of nine.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Spatial blur is governed by a different system response that was not\nchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>For multichannel, energy-resolving and ultrasound detectors, noise\nmodels, coherent signals, gain and temporal coupling differ. In\nultrasound, pulse-echo depth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=c\\,\\Delta t/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a time-of-flight relation, not a Poisson relation; sound speed and\nattenuation require separate assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The distinction between a measured signal and biological effect\nmatters for the following dosimetry lesson. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M40&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/w7qp0-nph35\">IAEA INIS – Digital Imaging, Chapter 16<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L3 – Digital Detectors, Poisson Noise and Image Quality · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/3edwt-0rf59\">IAEA INIS – Diagnostic radiology physics: handbook<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L3 – Digital Detectors, Poisson Noise and Image Quality · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M40-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M40-L4 – Absorbed Dose, Energy Deposition and Idealized Survival Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M40 – Master: Advanced Medical Physics and Quality Assurance<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M15: radiation interactions; M24:\nradiation protection; M40-L3.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply the definition of absorbed\ndose and properly delimit an abstract cell-survival model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>For ionizing radiation, energy deposition in matter is a physically\nmeasurable quantity. Dose distribution, calibration, biological response\nand clinical intention are nevertheless distinct layers of radiation\npractice. The survival model used here sketches a laboratory response;\nit is not a treatment-planning rule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Absorbed dose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis mean deposited energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{dep}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Gy}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA common phenomenological model of surviving fraction after a single\nexposure is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(D)=\\exp(-\\alpha D-\\beta D^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith material-dependent parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Gy^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Gy^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nParameters vary with cell type and conditions. Dose is not dose rate or\na radiation-weighted protection quantity.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\frac{E_{\\mathrm{dep}}}{m},\\qquad S(D)=\\exp(-\\alpha D-\\beta D^2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Illustrative energy deposition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{dep}=4\\,\\mathrm{mJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=2\\,\\mathrm{Gy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nChoosing <em>synthetic<\/em> cell-model parameters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.20\\,\\mathrm{Gy^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0.020\\,\\mathrm{Gy^{-2}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.48<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.619<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(2\\,\\mathrm{Gy})=e^{-0.48}\\approx0.619<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis says nothing about patients, tumors or treatment doses.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_237_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic exponential survival curve versus absorbed dose, with illustrative marker.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic exponential survival curve\nversus absorbed dose, with illustrative marker.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic survival curve as a function of absorbed dose.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The parameters are fictional; the figure contains no biological or\nclinical observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What dose results from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{mJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the example model give for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the cell model not be used as a clinical treatment\nrecommendation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Gy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It omits tissue heterogeneity, repair, fractionation, clinical data\nand many biological factors.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Examples are strictly educational. Clinical irradiation requires\ncalibrated instrumentation, independent checks, qualified personnel and\napplicable procedures; no limits or dose recommendations are derived\nhere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Physical measurement accuracy is only one part of medical evidence.\nThe next lesson analyzes diagnostic misclassification. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M40&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/zvrz8-q5q11\">IAEA INIS – Radiation Oncology Physics: A Handbook for Teachers and Students<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L4 – Absorbed Dose, Energy Deposition and Idealized Survival Models · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/sites/nss-oui/Published%20Chunks/m_60b8e698-4f59-4c24-809a-dfb819ac5050/c_78757eae-3d98-4ec4-8e35-ec24c3ff0dd9__9_0.Html\">IAEA – Radiation Protection and Safety in Medical Uses of Ionizing Radiation (SSG-46)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L4 – Absorbed Dose, Energy Deposition and Idealized Survival Models · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M40-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M40-L5 – Sensitivity, Specificity, Prevalence and Predictive Values<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M40 – Master: Advanced Medical Physics and Quality Assurance<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: probability; M32: experimental\ndesign; M40-L4.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Avoid confusing diagnostic\nmetrics and quantify prevalence effects in a complete numeric\nexample.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Image quality alone does not establish how well a classifier\ndistinguishes two disease states. Sensitivity conditions on cases;\nspecificity on non-cases. Positive predictive value instead asks what\nfraction of positive tests are true cases and therefore depends on\nprevalence. With a rare condition, many positive results may be false\neven for a useful test.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrue positives,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfalse negatives,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntrue negatives and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfalse positives,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Se}=TP/(TP+FN)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sp}=TN/(TN+FP)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBayes’ theorem with prevalence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{PPV}=\\mathrm{Se}\\pi/[\\mathrm{Se}\\pi+(1-\\mathrm{Sp})(1-\\pi)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese quantities only apply to a specified target condition, threshold,\ntest population and reference standard.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>π<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{PPV}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Se}\\,\\pi}{\\mathrm{Se}\\,\\pi+(1-\\mathrm{Sp})(1-\\pi)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>In a <em>synthetic<\/em> cohort of 2,000 people, 100 are positive by\nreference standard. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Se}=90\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>95<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sp}=95\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe counts are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TP=90<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FN=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1805<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TN=1805<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>95<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FP=95<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPositive predictive value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>95<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>48.6<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90/(90+95)\\approx48.6\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese values are a statistics exercise and do not describe any real\ntest.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_071_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two-by-two table with 90 true positives, 10 false negatives, 95 false positives and 1,805 true negatives.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two-by-two table with 90 true positives,\n10 false negatives, 95 false positives and 1,805 true\nnegatives.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Fictional 2,000-observation cohort.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>No patient data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many false positives remain for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>99<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sp}=99\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same 1,900 non-cases?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is negative predictive value in the baseline example?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does PPV depend on population even if sensitivity and\nspecificity stay fixed?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>19<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">19<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1805<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1805<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>99.45<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1805/(1805+10)\\approx99.45\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Changed prevalence alters the ratio of true to false positive\nresults.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Evaluation without uncertainty intervals overstates precision.\nVerification bias, imperfect reference standards, dependent observations\nand threshold selection change interpretation. Diagnostic metrics do not\nreplace clinical judgement for individuals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>These metrics also underpin independent validation of algorithmic\nimage analysis. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M40&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/statistical-guidance-reporting-results-studies-evaluating-diagnostic-tests-guidance-industry-and-fda\">FDA – Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L5 – Sensitivity, Specificity, Prevalence and Predictive Values · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/w7qp0-nph35\">IAEA INIS – Digital Imaging, Chapter 16<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L5 – Sensitivity, Specificity, Prevalence and Predictive Values · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M40-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M40-L6 – Regularized Image Reconstruction and External AI Validation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M40 – Master: Advanced Medical Physics and Quality Assurance<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31: inverse methods and numerical\noptimization; M40-L5.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate reconstruction priors\nand generalization of image-analysis models as separate issues.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Reconstruction and AI-driven image classification are different\ntasks. Reconstruction seeks plausible spatial information from\nmeasurements; diagnostic use additionally requires performance on\nrepresentative independent data. High scores on the training data or\nrelated samples do not establish reliability in other settings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A simple linear inverse model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=Ax+n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTikhonov-type regularization minimizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|Ax-y\\|_2^2+\\lambda R(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA penalty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan stabilize an ill-posed problem but may introduce smoothing or bias.\nIf an AI model trains on reconstructions, scanner changes, protocols,\nage distributions and patient overlap across dataset splits can distort\nevaluation. Case-level partitioning, external assessment and prospective\nmonitoring are separate requirements.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/munder><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo minsize=\"120%\" maxsize=\"120%\" stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{x}=\\arg\\min_x\\bigl\\{\\|Ax-y\\|_2^2+\\lambda R(x)\\bigr\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a purely mathematical single-pixel example, choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(x)=x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(x-2)+0.5x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=1.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncompared with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout regularization. This bias alone does not measure diagnostic\nutility. The figure shows separate flows for training, internal\nassessment and external validation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_168_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Separate pathways for training, internal testing and independent external validation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Separate pathways for training, internal\ntesting and independent external validation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Data-flow methodology sketch, not a certified medical-device\nprocess.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the same one-pixel example?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are images from one person across training and test sets\nproblematic?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is high PSNR not a substitute for diagnostic test\nperformance?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat x=2/(1+1)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It creates information leakage and an optimistic estimate of\ngeneralization.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A pixel similarity metric does not reliably capture clinically\nimportant errors.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>FDA guiding principles concern development practice, not automatic\napproval or legal clearance. A model can fail under distribution shift\neven with correct data partitioning. External validation must reflect\nthe intended environment and quantify uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The final lesson links measurement quality, traceability and\nrecurring tests. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M40&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jmri.24687\">Hansen &amp; Kellman – Image Reconstruction: An Overview for Clinicians<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L6 – Regularized Image Reconstruction and External AI Validation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/artificial-intelligence-enabled-medical-devices/good-machine-learning-practice-medical-device-development-guiding-principles\">FDA – Good Machine Learning Practice for Medical Device Development: Guiding Principles<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L6 – Regularized Image Reconstruction and External AI Validation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/statistical-guidance-reporting-results-studies-evaluating-diagnostic-tests-guidance-industry-and-fda\">FDA – Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L6 – Regularized Image Reconstruction and External AI Validation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M40-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M40-L7 – Acceptance, Commissioning and Uncertainty Budgets in Medical Physics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M40 – Master: Advanced Medical Physics and Quality Assurance<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M32: measurement uncertainty and\nexperimental design; M40-L1–L6.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish acceptance testing,\ncommissioning characterization and routine control, and combine simple\nuncertainties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Medical-physics systems can produce correct individual measurements\nyet fail under untested operating conditions. Initial acceptance,\ndetailed pre-use characterization and continuing quality control are\ntherefore distinct documented steps. Safety-critical uses require\ntraceable deviations and required independent checks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent uncorrelated relative standard uncertainties\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsufficiently linear error propagation permits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c=\\sqrt{\\sum_i u_i^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn expanded interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=k u_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith illustrative coverage factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not automatically a safety or acceptance threshold. Correlations\nrequire covariance terms; systematic biases require separate correction\nor assessment. Test limits come from applicable procedures, not this\nclassroom calculation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c=\\sqrt{u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2},\\qquad U=k\\,u_c.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With illustrative relative standard uncertainties\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_1=0.8\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_2=1.2\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_3=0.6\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe combination is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.64<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.44<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>%<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.56<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c=\\sqrt{0.64+1.44+0.36}\\%\\approx1.56\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFormally,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.12<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\approx3.12\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure distinguishes acceptance, commissioning and recurrent checks;\nthe numbers are not real-world tolerances.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_170_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Process chain from acceptance through commissioning to routine checks, with a schematic uncertainty budget.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Process chain from acceptance through\ncommissioning to routine checks, with a schematic uncertainty\nbudget.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic quality-assurance chain with an uncertainty\nbudget.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The diagram gives neither clinical tolerances nor device-specific\nauthorization recommendations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing only uncorrelated\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does passing acceptance not eliminate the need for recurring\ncalibration checks?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What changes if uncertainty components are correlated?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>1.2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>%<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.44<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{0.8^2+1.2^2}\\%\\approx1.44\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">System states and instruments may drift; ongoing checks detect later\nchanges.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Additional covariance terms can increase or decrease the combined\nuncertainty.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Without a specified measurement protocol, traceability, correlation\nmodel and documented acceptance criteria, the analysis cannot authorize\na system for service. It does not replace qualification, regulatory\nrequirements or independent clinical quality assurance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M40 closes the advanced medical-physics sequence from measurement\ndata to verifiable application.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/9s166-wzz43\">IAEA INIS – Acceptance Tests and Commissioning Measurements<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L7 – Acceptance, Commissioning and Uncertainty Budgets in Medical Physics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://inis.iaea.org/records/zvrz8-q5q11\">IAEA INIS – Radiation Oncology Physics: A Handbook for Teachers and Students<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L7 – Acceptance, Commissioning and Uncertainty Budgets in Medical Physics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/sites/nss-oui/Published%20Chunks/m_60b8e698-4f59-4c24-809a-dfb819ac5050/c_78757eae-3d98-4ec4-8e35-ec24c3ff0dd9__9_0.Html\">IAEA – Radiation Protection and Safety in Medical Uses of Ionizing Radiation (SSG-46)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Subject relevance to lesson: M40-L7 – Acceptance, Commissioning and Uncertainty Budgets in Medical Physics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
